WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.349
This view is called a mission arc .

00:00:02.349 --> 00:00:15.573
You picked a mission — say, the transfer from Earth to Mars — and the screen shows you the actual trajectory the spacecraft will fly, in heliocentric coordinates, against the orbits of the planets.

00:00:15.573 --> 00:00:16.647
Look at the arc.

00:00:16.647 --> 00:00:18.392
It is not a straight line.

00:00:18.392 --> 00:00:20.339
It cannot be a straight line.

00:00:20.339 --> 00:00:30.877
When the spacecraft leaves Earth, it leaves with Earth's velocity around the Sun — about thirty kilometers per second — plus whatever the rocket adds on top.

00:00:30.877 --> 00:00:36.113
That total velocity puts the spacecraft on a Keplerian ellipse around the Sun.

00:00:36.113 --> 00:00:39.469
The ellipse's perihelion sits at Earth's distance.

00:00:39.469 --> 00:00:44.034
Its aphelion reaches out to wherever it's going — Mars in this case.

00:00:44.034 --> 00:00:44.772
Press play.

00:00:44.772 --> 00:00:47.524
Watch the spacecraft slide along the arc.

00:00:47.524 --> 00:00:50.410
Watch Earth and Mars move at the same time.

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:58.130
Notice how the spacecraft's arrival has to be timed against Mars's own motion — Mars is moving while you're flying.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:07.259
If you leave a month too late, Mars will have moved on, and your arc — which is set by physics, not by you — will arrive at empty space.

00:01:07.259 --> 00:01:11.487
This is what the porkchop plot on the Plan screen is computing.

00:01:11.487 --> 00:01:19.072
For every possible launch date and arrival date, what does the arc cost in energy, and does the geometry line up?

00:01:19.072 --> 00:01:23.368
Some missions add a cislunar view — switchable from the toolbar.

00:01:23.368 --> 00:01:26.389
Cislunar is Earth-centered, not Sun-centered.

00:01:26.389 --> 00:01:38.404
It shows you what an Apollo crew or an Artemis crew sees: Earth in the middle, the Moon orbiting around, the spacecraft riding a trans-lunar injection arc to lunar orbit and back.

00:01:38.404 --> 00:01:45.788
Heliocentric arcs and cislunar arcs are two different geometries — same spacecraft, different reference frame.

00:01:45.788 --> 00:01:48.674
Use whichever makes the mission make sense.

00:01:48.674 --> 00:01:57.736
Below the view, the timeline lists the mission's phases — parking orbit, trans-Mars injection, cruise, mid-course corrections, arrival.

00:01:57.736 --> 00:01:59.146
Click any phase chip.

00:01:59.146 --> 00:02:02.502
The animation jumps to that moment in the mission.

00:02:02.502 --> 00:02:03.106
Pause it.

00:02:03.106 --> 00:02:04.247
Read the numbers.

00:02:04.247 --> 00:02:06.865
Restart and watch the next phase begin.

00:02:06.865 --> 00:02:11.496
Every mission you can fly here actually flew, or is scheduled to fly.

00:02:11.496 --> 00:02:12.570
The arc is real.

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:13.779
The clock is real.

00:02:13.779 --> 00:02:20.424
Only the speed has been adjusted, so a seven-month transfer fits on your screen in seventy seconds.

